NAME
       mkfs - create a filesystem

SYNOPSIS
       mkfs [-t fs] device [label]

DESCRIPTION
       Formats DEVICE. Without -t the type is chosen from the partition
       entry. It asks for confirmation first, since everything on the
       device is lost.

TYPES
       ext2    the default; simple and well exercised here
       ext4    extents, readable by Linux as a modern ext4 volume
       fat32   what an EFI system partition needs
       udf     read and written by Cervus, and readable by the udftools
               on Linux; the Linux kernel driver does not yet accept a
               volume made here

OPTIONS
       -t T   filesystem type to create

EXAMPLES
       mkfs -t ext4 /dev/sda2 root
       mkfs -t fat32 /dev/sda1 BOOT
       mkfs -t udf /dev/sdb1 DATA

SEE ALSO
       mkpart(1), mount(1), wipefs(1), fdisk(1)
